WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
the right patient group, and so we'll ultimately have to see.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:08.920
I think some of the other dynamics that will be important is cardiovascular versus stroke

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.000
and how those different elements contribute to the primary outcome.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.080
We do have a MACE IV endpoint here, so we do include stroke.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.400
And I think some of the recent literature would suggest stroke is going to be an important

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.440
element of the story for Lp level A, so we'll see how that unfolds.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.640
So exciting, but we'll also have to see.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.880
I do want to highlight as well that, as I've already said, we're going to be

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.640
looking at some of the other studies that have been articulated in the past.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.120
This will be a slow-building market simply because we need the testing rates to get much

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.120
higher.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.000
So this will be, assuming it all plays out as we hope, multiple iterations.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.640
But our DII-235 is ready to go into phase III studies, which could be a once-yearly

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.440
Lp level A injection, so we're quite excited about that as well to be ready.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.080
Lastly, I would note that ACC and AHA have now added that everybody in the United States

00:00:59.080 --> 00:00:59.680
should receive one dose of Lp level A.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:07.560
And that's a milestone to hopefully get testing rates up and to start to build the market

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.560
over time.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.560
So thanks for the question.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.560
Thanks so much.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.560
Thank you.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.760
Your next question comes from the line of Matthew Weston from UBS.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.760
Please go ahead.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.190
Thank you.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.190
My question is about Rapsido.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:30.430
Vas, now that we're heading to EU approval, Rapsido is going to be one of the first pricing

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:31.550
discussions for a multibillion dollar company.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:39.990
So assuming that you've had some early discussions with European governments, are you seeing

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.150
countries inclined to pay higher prices for innovation in Europe?

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.590
Yeah, thanks, Matthew.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:54.590
So first, it's important to note Rapsido only has an MFN impact for Medicaid because

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.950
the approval happened before the signing of our MFN agreements.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.230
Our first launch that we'll have, I think, clear MFN.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.670
Related implications across all segments in the U.S. would be Unalamab.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.630
So I think that's just one clarification.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.790
Nonetheless, I'll still answer the question in terms of the ongoing discussions with governments.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:18.070
We are engaging with governments across Europe as well as in Japan to hopefully get to a

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.450
better place.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.910
I think we're not seeing the progress that we had hoped to see at the pace that we had

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:24.910
hoped to see.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.330
So I think there has to be some urgency here because I do expect across the industry there

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.630
will be difficult decisions.

